; Linux io_uring backend, entirely Neo + direct kernel syscalls. The default
; on x86-64 (rt_uring_default); NEO_HTTP_URING=0 selects epoll, =1 opts in on
; ARM64. A worker whose ring cannot be created (kernels before 6.1, Docker's
; default seccomp profile) serves with epoll instead, silently unless =1.
; One ring per worker process, SINGLE_ISSUER | DEFER_TASKRUN: completions are
; posted only inside this worker's io_uring_enter. Each loop makes one
; io_uring_enter that submits every queued operation and waits for a
; completion. With a receive queued per request a connection has at most one
; operation in flight (a receive or a send), so a state is only dropped or
; reused when nothing references it; multishot receives (below) tag theirs.
; Accepts keep the epoll backend's round-robin turn: the listener (through a
; one-entry epoll instance) and the notification socket are polled through the
; ring, then accepted directly.
; user_data: listener poll=0, notification poll=1, receive=2+2i, send=3+2i
; (with the generation above bit 32 for multishot receives), 2^32-2 for the
; readiness poll (below) and 2^32-1 for cancels, whose completions are ignored.
;
; Ring descriptor (192 bytes): fd@0, mapping@8, sqes@16, sq mask@24,
; sq entries@32, sq tail@40, queued@48, cq mask@56, the kernel's offsets of
; sq tail@64, cq head@72, cq tail@80 and cqes@88, then cq head@96, and for
; multishot receives the buffer ring@104 (null without them), its buffers@112
; and the buffer ring tail this worker has filled up to@120. Then the
; readiness epoll@128 (-1 without one), its connections' interests@136, the
; connection metadata@144, its event buffer@152 and the sizes of the ring
; mapping@160 and of the submission entries@168.
; Ring indices are unsigned 32-bit counters kept as i64 in [0, 2^32).
;
; Timers, tasks, continuations and shutdown (http_server_options, intervals
; registered before serving) run as in the epoll backend. Each loop starts
; with rt_async_tick, and the wait's timeout is the time to the next timer,
; task or continuation (rt_async_timeout). What they wait for is readiness,
; so the worker keeps one epoll instance for it, polled through the ring like
; the listener's: task waits register there (it is the task backend,
; state@1768), the signal monitor is in it, and so is every connection a
; continuation or a render group owns (close 2). Such a connection is written
; from the tick with plain non-blocking sends, which a send in flight in the
; ring could not share its buffer with, so it leaves the ring for good when
; the request code hands it over (rt_uring_handoff) and is served from then on
; by the epoll backend's own rt_step. Ordinary requests never touch that
; epoll. After SIGINT or SIGTERM (rt_uring_draining) the listener closes, idle
; connections close, a response being sent finishes and closes, owned
; connections get the grace period, and the worker returns once none is left
; and no task is live.
;
; Multishot receives. Where the kernel takes a ring of provided buffers
; (IORING_REGISTER_PBUF_RING) each connection keeps one receive armed
; (IORING_RECV_MULTISHOT) instead of queuing one per request: each arrival
; completes into one of 128 1 KiB buffers, which is copied to the connection's
; input and handed back before the next io_uring_enter. With a receive queued
; per request, most found nothing yet (87% on hello with the server as the
; bottleneck), so the kernel tried it, armed a poll, woke it and tried again;
; the armed receive keeps one poll for the connection's lifetime. A connection
; then has its receive and at most one send in flight. Its slot's metadata
; (16 bytes at local@16: generation@0, flags@8 with 1 receive armed, 2 send
; in flight, 4 receive being cancelled, 8 handed to the readiness epoll) tags
; each operation's user_data with the generation, so a completion that
; arrives after its connection closed is recognised and only its buffer is
; returned. A connection is closed only
; with no send in flight (an end of input during a send closes it when the
; send completes); its armed receive is then cancelled, which also releases
; the kernel's reference to the socket. While a send is in flight, input that
; piles up past 64 KiB stops the receive until the connection catches up, as
; a full socket buffer would. NEO_HTTP_URING_MULTISHOT=0 queues a receive per
; request as before.
; Hello with the server capped at half a core (wrk -c64 on the other core, 9
; interleaved rounds on a 2-vCPU EPYC Genoa VM, Linux 6.8): 6% fewer
; instructions and cycles per request than with a receive queued per request
; (18.7k and 24.4k against 19.7k and 26.2k, medians). Every server measured
; there sends the same 2 TCP segments per request, and 97% of this server's
; profile is in the kernel, nearly all of it loopback TCP.
;
; Registered sockets (IOSQE_FIXED_FILE) are not used. Before Linux 6.13 the
; kernel frees a file removed from the ring's table only once every operation
; issued before the removal has completed, oldest first, so one keep-alive
; connection's armed receive held back every later closed connection's socket:
; no FIN reached its client (http-smoke's compaction case on 6.8). They would
; save each send's socket lookup, 1.5% of the profile.

effect fn rt_uring_setup (d : ptr) -> bool
  = let params = stack_alloc(120) in let cleared = memset(params, i32(0), 120) in
    ; CQSIZE | SUBMIT_ALL | SINGLE_ISSUER | DEFER_TASKRUN with 2048 completions:
    ; at most 1026 operations are ever in flight, so that queue never overflows.
    ; 256 submissions: rt_uring_push enters the ring when they are all queued,
    ; which posts no completion (DEFER_TASKRUN). Both queues are used round
    ; and round, so every page of them becomes resident: 2048 submissions and
    ; 4096 completions were 200 KiB of each worker's memory, these are 50.
    let sized = store_i32(params, 4, i32(2048)) in
    let flags = store_i32(params, 8, i32(12424)) in
    let fd = rt_result(syscall6(425, 256, ptr_to_i64(params), 0, 0, 0, 0)) in
    if fd < 0 then false else
      let features = i64(load_i32(params, 20)) in
      let sq_entries = i64(load_i32(params, 0)) in
      let cq_entries = i64(load_i32(params, 4)) in
      let array = i64(load_i32(params, 64)) in
      let sq_bytes = array + sq_entries * 4 in
      let cq_bytes = i64(load_i32(params, 100)) + cq_entries * 16 in
      ; One mapping for both rings (FEAT_SINGLE_MMAP) and timed waits (FEAT_EXT_ARG).
      let ring = if features % 2 == 1 && features / 256 % 2 == 1
        then rt_map_ring(fd, if sq_bytes > cq_bytes then sq_bytes else cq_bytes, 0) else -1 in
      let sqes = if ring < 0 then -1 else rt_map_ring(fd, sq_entries * 64, 268435456) in
      if sqes < 0 then let closed = rt_close(fd) in false else
        let mapping = ptr_from_i64(ring) in
        let identity = loop (i = 0) while i < sq_entries
          next (let slot = store_i32(mapping, array + i * 4, i32(i)) in i + 1) yield i in
        let a0 = store_i64(d, 0, fd) in
        let a1 = store_ptr(d, 8, mapping) in
        let a2 = store_ptr(d, 16, ptr_from_i64(sqes)) in
        let a3 = store_i64(d, 24, sq_entries - 1) in
        let a4 = store_i64(d, 32, sq_entries) in
        let a5 = store_i64(d, 40, bit_and(i64(atomic_load_i32(mapping, i64(load_i32(params, 44)))), 4294967295)) in
        let a6 = store_i64(d, 48, 0) in
        let a7 = store_i64(d, 56, cq_entries - 1) in
        let a8 = store_i64(d, 64, i64(load_i32(params, 44))) in
        let a9 = store_i64(d, 72, i64(load_i32(params, 80))) in
        let a10 = store_i64(d, 80, i64(load_i32(params, 84))) in
        let a11 = store_i64(d, 88, i64(load_i32(params, 100))) in
        let a12 = store_i64(d, 96, bit_and(i64(atomic_load_i32(mapping, i64(load_i32(params, 80)))), 4294967295)) in
        let a13 = store_i64(d, 160, if sq_bytes > cq_bytes then sq_bytes else cq_bytes) in
        let a14 = store_i64(d, 168, sq_entries * 64) in true

; The buffer ring (one page of 128 16-byte entries: address, length, buffer id,
; with the tail in the last two bytes of entry 0) and its buffers, registered
; as buffer group 0. Leaves d@104 null when the kernel refuses it.
effect fn rt_uring_buffers (d : ptr) -> unit
  = let off = rt_getenv(data("NEO_HTTP_URING_MULTISHOT\0")) in
    let base = if off != null() && load_u8(off, 0) == '0' then -1 else rt_private_map(4096 + 131072) in
    let none = store_ptr(d, 104, null()) in
    if base <= 0 then noop() else
      let ring = ptr_from_i64(base) in
      let register = stack_alloc(40) in let cleared = memset(register, i32(0), 40) in
      let address = store_i64(register, 0, base) in let entries = store_i32(register, 8, i32(128)) in
      ; IORING_REGISTER_PBUF_RING. Ubuntu 6.8.0 kernels (seen on 6.8.0-147) invert
      ; the reserved-field check and refuse it zeroed with EINVAL, so a refused
      ; registration is retried once with reserved[0] set, which they accept and
      ; correct kernels never see.
      let registered = rt_uring_register_buffers(d, register) in
      if registered < 0 then let unmapped = rt_munmap(ring, 4096 + 131072) in noop() else
        let buffers = store_ptr(d, 112, ptr_add(ring, 4096)) in let tail = store_i64(d, 120, 0) in
        let filled = loop (i = 0) while i < 128 next (let given = rt_uring_provide(ring, d, i) in i + 1) yield i in
        let published = rt_uring_publish(ring, d) in store_ptr(d, 104, ring)

effect fn rt_uring_register_buffers (d : ptr, register : ptr) -> i64
  = let first = syscall6(427, load_i64(d, 0), 22, ptr_to_i64(register), 1, 0, 0) in
    if first != -22 then rt_result(first) else
      let marked = store_i64(register, 16, 1) in
      rt_result(syscall6(427, load_i64(d, 0), 22, ptr_to_i64(register), 1, 0, 0))

; Puts buffer bid at the filled tail; rt_uring_publish shows it to the kernel.
; There is no 16-bit store, so an entry's id is written with a 32-bit store:
; in entry 0 its upper half is the published tail, which it keeps.
effect fn rt_uring_provide (ring : ptr, d : ptr, bid : i64) -> unit
  = let tail = load_i64(d, 120) in let slot = bit_and(tail, 127) in let entry = ptr_add(ring, slot * 16) in
    let address = store_i64(entry, 0, ptr_to_i64(ptr_add(load_ptr(d, 112), bid * 1024))) in
    let length = store_i32(entry, 8, i32(1024)) in
    let shown = bit_and(i64(load_i32(ring, 12)), 4294901760) in
    let id = store_i32(entry, 12, i32(if slot == 0 then bit_or(bid, shown) else bid)) in
    store_i64(d, 120, bit_and(tail + 1, 65535))
effect fn rt_uring_publish (ring : ptr, d : ptr) -> unit
  = atomic_store_i32(ring, 12, i32(bit_or(bit_and(i64(load_i32(ring, 12)), 65535), shift_left(load_i64(d, 120), 16))))

; Publishes the queued submissions and enters the ring once. With a wait
; argument (a 24-byte io_uring_getevents_arg) it also waits for a completion,
; up to the argument's timeout. Returns the submitted count or -1 (errno set).
effect fn rt_uring_submit (d : ptr, waiting : ptr) -> i64
  = let published = atomic_store_i32(load_ptr(d, 8), load_i64(d, 64), i32(load_i64(d, 40))) in
    let queued = load_i64(d, 48) in
    let wait = waiting != null() in
    ; IORING_ENTER_GETEVENTS | IORING_ENTER_EXT_ARG when waiting.
    let result = rt_result(syscall6(426, load_i64(d, 0), queued, if wait then 1 else 0,
      if wait then 9 else 0, ptr_to_i64(waiting), if wait then 24 else 0)) in
    let consumed = if result > 0 && queued > 0 then store_i64(d, 48, queued - result) else noop() in result

; Queues one submission: opcode@0, fd@4, addr@16, len@24, op flags@28, user_data@32.
effect fn rt_uring_push (d : ptr, opcode : i64, fd : i64, address : i64, length : i64, flags : i64, user : i64) -> unit
  = let room = if load_i64(d, 48) < load_i64(d, 32) then noop() else
      let submitted = rt_uring_submit(d, null()) in
      if load_i64(d, 48) < load_i64(d, 32) then noop() else ax_trap(data("io_uring submission queue full\0")) in
    let tail = load_i64(d, 40) in
    let sqe = ptr_add(load_ptr(d, 16), bit_and(tail, load_i64(d, 24)) * 64) in
    let w0 = store_i64(sqe, 0, bit_or(opcode, shift_left(fd, 32))) in
    let w1 = store_i64(sqe, 8, 0) in
    let w2 = store_i64(sqe, 16, address) in
    let w3 = store_i64(sqe, 24, bit_or(length, shift_left(flags, 32))) in
    let w4 = store_i64(sqe, 32, user) in
    let w5 = store_i64(sqe, 40, 0) in let w6 = store_i64(sqe, 48, 0) in let w7 = store_i64(sqe, 56, 0) in
    let advanced = store_i64(d, 40, bit_and(tail + 1, 4294967295)) in
    store_i64(d, 48, load_i64(d, 48) + 1)

; IORING_OP_RECV into the free input tail, attempted when it is submitted. A
; receive is queued after its connection's response went out, and under load
; the client's next request has usually arrived by the next submission, so the
; receive completes inline instead of arming a poll first (POLL_FIRST); when
; nothing has arrived the kernel arms the same poll after the attempt. With the
; server as the bottleneck (hello, server on its own core, wrk -t2 on another)
; this measured 3% more requests per second and 3% less CPU per request on one
; EPYC-Milan box and no difference beyond run-to-run noise on a second one
; (RESULTS-zig-x86-linux-2026-11-02.md). (With POLL_FIRST an earlier
; measurement on a client-bound setup saw 0.25 us less CPU per request.)
effect fn rt_uring_recv (d : ptr, state : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let room = rt_http_input_room(state) in let have = load_i64(state, 16) in
    rt_uring_push(d, 27, load_i64(state, 0),
      ptr_to_i64(ptr_add(load_ptr(state, 8), have)), room, 0, 2 + index * 2)

; IORING_OP_SEND of the unsent output, MSG_NOSIGNAL.
effect fn rt_uring_send (d : ptr, state : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let sent = load_i64(state, 40) in
    rt_uring_push(d, 26, load_i64(state, 0), ptr_to_i64(ptr_add(load_ptr(state, 24), sent)),
      load_i64(state, 32) - sent, 16384, 3 + index * 2)

; Renders every complete buffered request, then sends the responses or reads more.
effect fn rt_uring_continue (d : ptr, state : ptr, handler : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let prepared = if load_i64(state, 16) > 0 then rt_prepare(state, handler) else noop() in
    if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then noop()
    else if load_i64(state, 48) == 2 then rt_uring_handoff(d, ptr_add(load_ptr(d, 144), index * 16), state, index)
    else if load_i64(state, 32) > 0 then rt_uring_send(d, state, index)
    else rt_uring_recv(d, state, index)

effect fn rt_uring_complete (d : ptr, state : ptr, handler : ptr, index : i64, sending : bool, result : i64) -> unit
  = if result < 0 then
      if result == 0 - 4 || result == 0 - 11 then
        if sending then rt_uring_send(d, state, index) else rt_uring_recv(d, state, index)
      else rt_drop(state)
    else if result == 0 then rt_drop(state)
    else if sending then
      let progress = store_i64(state, 72, rt_http_now()) in
      let sent = load_i64(state, 40) + result in
      if sent < load_i64(state, 32) then let saved = store_i64(state, 40, sent) in rt_uring_send(d, state, index)
      else let reset = store_i64(state, 32, 0) in let rewound = store_i64(state, 40, 0) in
        let close = load_i64(state, 48) in
        if close == 1 then rt_drop(state)
        else if close == 3 then let lingering = rt_http_linger(state) in rt_uring_recv(d, state, index)
        else rt_uring_continue(d, state, handler, index)
    else if load_i64(state, 48) == 4 then
      ; Lingering after a refused request: discard what arrives, up to 1 MiB.
      let total = load_i64(state, 120) + result in
      if total > 1048576 then rt_drop(state) else let counted = store_i64(state, 120, total) in rt_uring_recv(d, state, index)
    else let before = load_i64(state, 16) in let received = store_i64(state, 16, before + result) in
      let stamped = rt_http_received(state, before) in
      rt_uring_continue(d, state, handler, index)

; ---- multishot receives (see the top): meta is the connection's 16 bytes.
; user_data: 2+2i for the receive, 3+2i for a send, the generation above bit 32.
effect fn rt_uring_user (meta : ptr, index : i64, kind : i64) -> i64
  = 2 + index * 2 + kind + shift_left(bit_and(load_i64(meta, 0), 2147483647), 32)

; IORING_OP_RECV | IOSQE_BUFFER_SELECT with IORING_RECV_MULTISHOT, buffer group 0.
effect fn rt_uring_arm (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let armed = store_i64(meta, 8, bit_or(load_i64(meta, 8), 1)) in
    rt_uring_push(d, 27 + 32 * 256 + 2 * 65536, load_i64(state, 0), 0, 0, 0, rt_uring_user(meta, index, 0))

effect fn rt_uring_send_armed (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let sending = store_i64(meta, 8, bit_or(load_i64(meta, 8), 2)) in let sent = load_i64(state, 40) in
    rt_uring_push(d, 26, load_i64(state, 0), ptr_to_i64(ptr_add(load_ptr(state, 24), sent)),
      load_i64(state, 32) - sent, 16384, rt_uring_user(meta, index, 1))

; IORING_OP_ASYNC_CANCEL of the armed receive with IOSQE_CQE_SKIP_SUCCESS. Its
; own completion (only when the receive had already ended) is ignored. Flag 4
; holds off a new receive until the old one's last completion arrives: both
; carry the same user_data, and two receives on one socket could split its data.
effect fn rt_uring_cancel (d : ptr, meta : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = if bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), 1) == 0 then noop() else
      let disarmed = store_i64(meta, 8, bit_or(bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), -2), 4)) in
      rt_uring_push(d, 14 + 64 * 256, 0, rt_uring_user(meta, index, 0), 0, 0, 4294967295)

; The connection was closed (by rt_drop here or in the request code): stop its
; receive and retire its generation.
effect fn rt_uring_retire (d : ptr, meta : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let cancelled = rt_uring_cancel(d, meta, index) in
    let aged = store_i64(meta, 0, load_i64(meta, 0) + 1) in store_i64(meta, 8, 0)
effect fn rt_uring_close (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = if bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), 2) != 0 then store_i64(state, 48, 1) else
      let dropped = rt_drop(state) in rt_uring_retire(d, meta, index)

; The request code made a continuation or a render group the connection's
; owner (close 2). Nothing of it is in flight in the ring but its armed
; receive: that is cancelled and the generation retired, so whatever the
; receive still completes with only returns its buffer (what a client sends
; to an owned connection is discarded anyway). Flag 8 marks the slot until a
; new connection is accepted into it. The socket becomes non-blocking, what is
; queued is sent as far as it goes, and the readiness epoll takes the
; connection with the epoll backend's interests.
effect fn rt_uring_handoff (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let retired = rt_uring_retire(d, meta, index) in let marked = store_i64(meta, 8, 8) in
    let epoll = load_i64(d, 128) in
    if epoll < 0 || rt_nonblock(load_i64(state, 0)) < 0 then rt_drop(state) else
      let flushed = if load_i64(state, 32) > 0 then rt_flush(state) else noop() in
      if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then noop() else
        let wanted = rt_epoll_wanted(state) in
        let added = rt_epoll_apply(epoll, 1, load_i64(state, 0), wanted, rt_http_token(index)) in
        store_i64(load_ptr(d, 136), index * 8, wanted)

; With nothing being sent: render what is buffered, then send it, or keep
; receiving.
effect fn rt_uring_next (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, handler : ptr, index : i64) -> unit
  = let prepared = if load_i64(state, 16) > 0 && load_i64(state, 48) < 4 then rt_prepare(state, handler) else noop() in
    if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then rt_uring_retire(d, meta, index)
    else if load_i64(state, 48) == 2 then rt_uring_handoff(d, meta, state, index)
    else if load_i64(state, 32) > 0 then rt_uring_send_armed(d, meta, state, index)
    else if bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), 5) == 0 then rt_uring_arm(d, meta, state, index) else noop()

; Appends from source while the input can grow; the bytes left, or -1 when the
; input is at its limit.
effect fn rt_uring_append (state : ptr, source : ptr, left : i64) -> i64
  = let room = rt_http_input_room(state) in if room <= 0 then -1 else
      let count = if room < left then room else left in let have = load_i64(state, 16) in
      let copied = memcpy(ptr_add(load_ptr(state, 8), have), source, count) in
      let saved = store_i64(state, 16, have + count) in left - count

; n bytes arrived in buffer bid: appended to the input (counted while
; lingering), and the buffer goes back. False when the input is full.
effect fn rt_uring_take (d : ptr, state : ptr, bid : i64, n : i64) -> bool
  = let source = ptr_add(load_ptr(d, 112), bid * 1024) in
    let before = load_i64(state, 16) in
    let left = if load_i64(state, 48) == 4 then let counted = store_i64(state, 120, load_i64(state, 120) + n) in 0
      else loop (left = n) while left > 0 next (rt_uring_append(state, ptr_add(source, n - left), left)) yield left in
    let given = rt_uring_provide(load_ptr(d, 104), d, bid) in
    let stamped = if left == 0 && load_i64(state, 48) != 4 then rt_http_received(state, before) else noop() in left == 0

effect fn rt_uring_received (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, handler : ptr, index : i64, result : i64, flags : i64) -> unit
  = let ended = if bit_and(flags, 2) != 0 then noop() else store_i64(meta, 8, bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), -6)) in
    let sending = bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), 2) != 0 in
    if result > 0 then
      if bit_and(flags, 1) == 0 || !rt_uring_take(d, state, bit_and(shift_right(flags, 16), 65535), result) then rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, index)
      else if load_i64(state, 48) == 4 then
        (if load_i64(state, 120) > 1048576 then rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, index) else rt_uring_next(d, meta, state, handler, index))
      else if !sending then rt_uring_next(d, meta, state, handler, index)
      else if load_i64(state, 16) > 65536 then rt_uring_cancel(d, meta, index) else noop()
    ; Out of buffers (ENOBUFS), interrupted, or cancelled for backpressure: armed
    ; again once nothing is being sent.
    else if result == 0 - 105 || result == 0 - 4 || result == 0 - 11 || result == 0 - 125 then
      (if sending then noop() else rt_uring_next(d, meta, state, handler, index))
    else rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, index)

effect fn rt_uring_sent (d : ptr, meta : ptr, state : ptr, handler : ptr, index : i64, result : i64) -> unit
  = let done = store_i64(meta, 8, bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), -3)) in
    if result < 0 then
      if result == 0 - 4 || result == 0 - 11 then rt_uring_send_armed(d, meta, state, index) else rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, index)
    else if result == 0 then rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, index)
    else
      let progress = store_i64(state, 72, rt_http_now()) in
      let sent = load_i64(state, 40) + result in
      if sent < load_i64(state, 32) then let saved = store_i64(state, 40, sent) in rt_uring_send_armed(d, meta, state, index)
      else let reset = store_i64(state, 32, 0) in let rewound = store_i64(state, 40, 0) in
        let close = load_i64(state, 48) in
        if close == 1 then rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, index)
        else let lingering = if close == 3 then rt_http_linger(state) else noop() in
          rt_uring_next(d, meta, state, handler, index)

; A completion for connection index under the generation in user's upper half;
; one for a closed connection, or a failed cancel, only returns its buffer.
effect fn rt_uring_armed_event (d : ptr, states : ptr, local : ptr, handler : ptr, user : i64, result : i64, flags : i64) -> unit
  = let low = bit_and(user, 4294967295) in let index = (low - 2) / 2 in
    let meta = ptr_add(load_ptr(local, 16), index * 16) in let state = ptr_add(states, index * 128) in
    if low == 4294967295 || index < 0 || index >= load_i64(local, 0) || shift_right(user, 32) != bit_and(load_i64(meta, 0), 2147483647)
      || load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then
      (if bit_and(flags, 1) != 0 then rt_uring_provide(load_ptr(d, 104), d, bit_and(shift_right(flags, 16), 65535)) else noop())
    else if low % 2 == 1 then rt_uring_sent(d, meta, state, handler, index, result)
    else rt_uring_received(d, meta, state, handler, index, result, flags)

; The readiness epoll has events: task waits, the signal monitor, and the
; connections handed to it, stepped as the epoll backend steps them. It is
; level-triggered, so events beyond this batch raise its poll again.
effect fn rt_uring_ready (d : ptr, states : ptr, n : i64, handler : ptr, control : ptr) -> unit
  = let epoll = load_i64(d, 128) in let events = load_ptr(d, 152) in
    let ready = rt_epoll_wait_timeout(epoll, events, 32, 0) in
    let served = loop (i = 0) while i < ready
      next (let token = load_i64(events, i * rt_epoll_event_bytes() + rt_epoll_data_offset()) in
            let mask = i64(load_i32(events, i * rt_epoll_event_bytes())) in
            let stepped = if bit_and(token, 4095) >= 2048 then rt_fiber_notify(token)
              else if token == 1026 then rt_http_shutdown_signal(control, load_i64(runtime_state(), 1696) - 1)
              else let index = rt_http_token_index(token) in
                if index < 0 || index >= n then ax_trap(data("invalid epoll token\0")) else
                  let state = ptr_add(states, index * 128) in
                  ; Only a connection that was handed over: a closed one's socket can
                  ; outlive it in a forked child, and its slot may be in the ring again.
                  if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 || bit_and(load_i64(load_ptr(d, 144), index * 16 + 8), 8) == 0 then noop() else
                    let done = rt_step(state, mask, handler) in
                    if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then noop() else rt_epoll_sync(epoll, state, load_ptr(d, 136), index) in i + 1)
      yield i in noop()

; After a tick with continuations or tasks live: what they queued or finished
; changes what their connections wait for.
effect fn rt_uring_sync (d : ptr, states : ptr, n : i64) -> unit
  = let epoll = load_i64(d, 128) in let meta = load_ptr(d, 144) in
    let synced = loop (i = 0) while i < n
      next (let state = ptr_add(states, i * 128) in
            let sync = if load_i64(state, 0) >= 0 && bit_and(load_i64(meta, i * 16 + 8), 8) != 0
              then rt_epoll_sync(epoll, state, load_ptr(d, 136), i) else noop() in i + 1)
      yield i in noop()

; Shutdown, as rt_http_draining for the epoll backend: timers stop, the
; listener closes, and the worker is done once no connection and no task is
; left. A connection the readiness epoll serves is treated as there: closed
; when it has nothing to send and no owner, or when the grace period is over.
; In the ring an idle connection closes at once and one whose response is
; being sent closes when the send completes; a receive queued per request
; (without multishot receives) is ended by shutting the socket down, as the
; sweep does. Past the grace period a send in flight is ended the same way.
effect fn rt_uring_draining (d : ptr, control : ptr, states : ptr, n : i64, listener : i64) -> bool
  = let deadline = atomic_load_i64(control, 8) in if deadline <= 0 then false else
    let timers = rt_timer_stop_all() in
    let closed = if load_i64(runtime_state(), 1704) == 0 then let closed = rt_close(listener) in store_i64(runtime_state(), 1704, 1) else noop() in
    let expired = rt_clock_ns() / 1000000 >= deadline in
    let multishot = load_ptr(d, 104) != null() in
    let active = loop (i = 0, active = 0) while i < n next (i + 1,
      let state = ptr_add(states, i * 128) in let meta = ptr_add(load_ptr(d, 144), i * 16) in
      if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then active else
      if bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), 8) != 0 then
        (if expired || load_i64(state, 32) == 0 && load_i64(state, 48) != 2 then let dropped = rt_drop(state) in active else
         let close = if load_i64(state, 48) == 2 then noop() else store_i64(state, 48, 1) in active + 1)
      else if multishot && bit_and(load_i64(meta, 8), 2) == 0 then let gone = rt_uring_close(d, meta, state, i) in active
      else
        let sending = multishot || load_i64(state, 32) > 0 in
        let close = if sending then store_i64(state, 48, 1) else noop() in
        let ended = if (sending && !expired) || bit_and(load_i64(state, 112), 4) != 0 then noop() else
          let marked = store_i64(state, 112, bit_or(load_i64(state, 112), 4)) in
          let shut = rt_shutdown_both(load_i64(state, 0)) in noop() in active + 1) yield active in
    active == 0 && load_i64(runtime_state(), 1784) == 0

; Timeouts: a connection always has an operation in flight, so an expired one
; is shut down, and that operation's completion closes it.
effect fn rt_uring_sweep (states : ptr, n : i64, control : ptr, parent : i64) -> unit
  = let orphaned = if load_i64(runtime_state(), 2024) != 0 && rt_getppid() != parent then ax_exit(0) else noop() in
    let now = rt_http_now() in
    let swept = loop (i = 0) while i < n
      next (let state = ptr_add(states, i * 128) in let action = rt_http_expiry(state, now, control) in
        let expired = if action == 0 then noop() else
          let answered = if action == 2 then rt_http_refuse_now(load_i64(state, 0), 408) else noop() in
          let marked = store_i64(state, 112, bit_or(load_i64(state, 112), 4)) in
          let shut = rt_shutdown_both(load_i64(state, 0)) in noop() in i + 1)
      yield i in noop()

; First dropped state below the count, else the count (rt_accept_at refuses
; slots at the connection limit).
effect fn rt_uring_slot (states : ptr, n : i64) -> i64
  = loop (i = 0, found = -1) while found < 0 && i < n
      next (i + 1, if load_i64(states, i * 128) < 0 then i else -1)
      yield if found < 0 then n else found

effect fn rt_uring_event (d : ptr, states : ptr, local : ptr, handler : ptr, listener : i64, control : ptr,
                          worker : i64, workers : i64{workers >= 1}, scratch : ptr, user : i64, result : i64, flags : i64) -> unit
  = if user == 0 then
      ; Accept every pending connection while this worker holds the accept
      ; turn (with one worker, until the listener is drained). One accept per
      ; readiness event made the k-th of a burst of new connections wait out k
      ; loop iterations, each serving the requests of those already accepted.
      let rearm = store_i64(local, 8, 0) in
      let drained = loop (more = atomic_load_i64(control, 8) == 0) while more
        next (let slot = rt_accept_at(listener, states, rt_uring_slot(states, load_i64(local, 0)), true,
                local, control, worker, workers) in
              if slot < 0 then false else
              let fresh = store_i64(ptr_add(load_ptr(local, 16), slot * 16), 8, 0) in
              let queued = if load_ptr(d, 104) == null() then rt_uring_recv(d, ptr_add(states, slot * 128), slot)
                else rt_uring_arm(d, ptr_add(load_ptr(local, 16), slot * 16), ptr_add(states, slot * 128), slot) in true)
        yield more in noop()
    else if user == 1 then
      let notify = load_i64(control, 256 + worker * 16) in
      let drained = rt_read(notify, scratch, 64) in
      let noted = rt_async_notified() in
      rt_uring_push(d, 6, notify, 0, 0, 1, 1)
    else if user == 4294967294 then
      let served = rt_uring_ready(d, states, load_i64(local, 0), handler, control) in
      rt_uring_push(d, 6, load_i64(d, 128), 0, 0, 1, 4294967294)
    else if load_ptr(d, 104) != null() then rt_uring_armed_event(d, states, local, handler, user, result, flags)
    else let index = (user - 2) / 2 in
      if index < 0 || index >= load_i64(local, 0) then ax_trap(data("invalid io_uring completion\0")) else
        let state = ptr_add(states, index * 128) in
        if load_i64(state, 0) < 0 then ax_trap(data("io_uring completion for a closed connection\0"))
        else rt_uring_complete(d, state, handler, index, user % 2 == 1, result)

; Handles every posted completion, then releases their slots to the kernel.
effect fn rt_uring_reap (d : ptr, states : ptr, local : ptr, handler : ptr, listener : i64, control : ptr,
                         worker : i64, workers : i64{workers >= 1}, scratch : ptr) -> unit
  = let mapping = load_ptr(d, 8) in
    let tail = bit_and(i64(atomic_load_i32(mapping, load_i64(d, 80))), 4294967295) in
    let head = loop (h = load_i64(d, 96)) while h != tail
      next (let cqe = ptr_add(mapping, load_i64(d, 88) + bit_and(h, load_i64(d, 56)) * 16) in
            let handled = rt_uring_event(d, states, local, handler, listener, control, worker, workers, scratch,
              load_i64(cqe, 0), i64(load_i32(cqe, 8)), i64(load_i32(cqe, 12))) in bit_and(h + 1, 4294967295))
      yield h in
    ; The buffers handed back while handling them are visible from the next entry.
    let given = if load_ptr(d, 104) == null() then noop() else rt_uring_publish(load_ptr(d, 104), d) in
    let saved = store_i64(d, 96, head) in
    atomic_store_i32(mapping, load_i64(d, 72), i32(head))

effect fn rt_http_uring_worker (d : ptr, listener : i64, handler : ptr, control : ptr, worker : i64,
                              workers : i64{workers >= 1}, parent : i64) -> i64
  = let states = ax_alloc(rt_http_limit(control) * 128) in
    ; local: connection count@0, listener polled@8, connection metadata@16.
    let local = stack_alloc(24) in let cleared = memset(local, i32(0), 24) in
    let meta = ax_alloc(rt_http_limit(control) * 16) in let unused = memset(meta, i32(0), rt_http_limit(control) * 16) in
    let kept = store_ptr(local, 16, meta) in let shared = store_ptr(d, 144, meta) in
    let scratch = stack_alloc(64) in
    let notify = load_i64(control, 256 + worker * 16) in
    let watched = rt_uring_push(d, 6, notify, 0, 0, 1, 1) in
    ; The ring polls the listener through this epoll instance, not directly: a
    ; pending ring request keeps its file open until the ring's teardown, which
    ; runs asynchronously after exit, so a server restarted on the port failed to
    ; bind for a few milliseconds. Epoll holds no reference, and the port is free
    ; as soon as the process exits. Without epoll the listener is polled directly.
    let gate = rt_epoll_create() in
    let target = if gate < 0 then listener else if rt_epoll_ctl(gate, 1, listener, 1, 0) >= 0 then gate
      else let closed = rt_close(gate) in listener in
    ; The readiness epoll (see the top). A server with options cannot do without it.
    let signal = load_i64(runtime_state(), 1696) - 1 in
    let epoll = rt_epoll_create() in let readiness = store_i64(d, 128, epoll) in
    let required = if epoll < 0 && signal >= 0 then ax_trap(data("epoll_create1 syscall failed\0")) else noop() in
    let interests = ax_alloc(rt_http_limit(control) * 8) in let i0 = store_ptr(d, 136, interests) in
    let events = ax_alloc(32 * rt_epoll_event_bytes()) in let e0 = store_ptr(d, 152, events) in
    let polled = if epoll < 0 then noop() else
      let backend = store_i64(runtime_state(), 1768, epoll + 1) in
      let monitored = if signal < 0 then noop() else rt_epoll_apply(epoll, 1, signal, 1, 1026) in
      rt_uring_push(d, 6, epoll, 0, 0, 1, 4294967294) in
    ; Waits end at the next timer, task or continuation, and after a second at
    ; the latest (a tenth with a signal monitor), so orphaned workers still
    ; notice and exit.
    let timeout = stack_alloc(16) in let seconds = store_i64(timeout, 0, 1) in let nanos = store_i64(timeout, 8, 0) in
    let waiting = stack_alloc(24) in let zeroed = memset(waiting, i32(0), 24) in
    let limit = store_i64(waiting, 16, ptr_to_i64(timeout)) in
    let served = loop (running = true) while running
      next (let pending = load_i64(runtime_state(), 1640) > 0 || load_i64(runtime_state(), 1784) > 0 in
            let ticks = rt_async_tick(states, load_i64(local, 0)) in
            let draining = if signal < 0 then false else rt_uring_draining(d, control, states, load_i64(local, 0), listener) in
            if draining then false else
            let synced = if !pending then noop() else rt_uring_sync(d, states, load_i64(local, 0)) in
            let swept = if rt_http_sweep_due() then rt_uring_sweep(states, load_i64(local, 0), control, parent) else noop() in
            let listening = if load_i64(control, 0) == worker && load_i64(local, 8) == 0 && atomic_load_i64(control, 8) == 0 then
              let polled = rt_uring_push(d, 6, target, 0, 0, 1, 0) in store_i64(local, 8, 1) else noop() in
            let delay = rt_async_timeout(if signal < 0 then 1000 else 100) in
            let whole = store_i64(timeout, 0, delay / 1000) in let part = store_i64(timeout, 8, delay % 1000 * 1000000) in
            let entered = rt_uring_submit(d, waiting) in
            let error = if entered < 0 then i64(load_i32(rt_errno(), 0)) else 0 in
            let tick = rt_http_tick() in
            ; ETIME, EINTR, EAGAIN and EBUSY leave the ring usable.
            if error != 0 && error != 62 && error != 4 && error != 11 && error != 16 then
              let failed = ax_trap(data("io_uring_enter syscall failed\0")) in false
            else let reaped = rt_uring_reap(d, states, local, handler, listener, control, worker, workers, scratch) in true)
      yield 0 in
    ; Shut down: the queued cancels go out, and closing the ring ends whatever
    ; is still in it and lets go of its sockets.
    let flushed = rt_uring_submit(d, null()) in
    let backend = store_i64(runtime_state(), 1768, 0) in
    let c0 = rt_close(load_i64(d, 0)) in
    let c1 = if epoll < 0 then 0 else rt_close(epoll) in let c2 = if gate < 0 || target != gate then 0 else rt_close(gate) in
    let u0 = rt_munmap(load_ptr(d, 16), load_i64(d, 168)) in let u1 = rt_munmap(load_ptr(d, 8), load_i64(d, 160)) in
    let u2 = if load_ptr(d, 104) == null() then 0 else rt_munmap(load_ptr(d, 104), 4096 + 131072) in
    let r0 = ax_release(states) in let r1 = ax_release(meta) in let r2 = ax_release(interests) in let r3 = ax_release(events) in served

effect fn rt_serve_uring (listener : i64, handler : ptr, control : ptr, worker : i64,
                        workers : i64{workers >= 1}, parent : i64, requested : bool) -> i64
  = let d = stack_alloc(192) in let blank = memset(d, i32(0), 192) in
    if rt_uring_setup(d) then let buffered = rt_uring_buffers(d) in rt_http_uring_worker(d, listener, handler, control, worker, workers, parent)
    else let noted = if requested && worker == 0 then rt_write_all(2, data("neo: io_uring unavailable, serving with epoll\n"), 46) else noop() in
      rt_http_epoll_worker(listener, handler, control, worker, workers, parent)
